# tree.mask - what tests/toolhelp/thcmp.py leaves out when it compares
# the probe's log on w16's install tree with w16's TOOLHELP against its
# log on Windows 3.1 with Microsoft's (tests/toolhelp/thcase.sh, parts
# tree286 and tree386; ARCHITECTURE.md 22). Every line here is a
# difference of w16's own modules (its KERNEL, USER, GDI, drivers, fonts),
# not of TOOLHELP: TOOLHELP reports what is there, and section d shows
# that Microsoft's TOOLHELP reports the same on w16's tree.
#
# ---- the global heap (section g) ----
# how many blocks there are, and which are in the LRU list: other modules,
# other blocks
^walk (ALL|LRU): size!=GlobalSize 0, address!=base [0-9]+$
^walk LRU: first
# a program's module table: fixed under w16's KRNL286 (wcLock 4Dh, its
# handle its selector) and of another size (w16 keeps no copy of the
# resident names it does not use)
^ours: MODULE data=0 size=
^GlobalEntryHandle\(own module\)
# w16 keeps a copy of a program's DGROUP as loaded, owned by its module
# (kernel/loader.c, for further instances); Windows 3.1 has a 48-byte
# (KRNL386: 32-byte) fixed block of the task's instead (UNVERIFIED what)
^ours: UNKNOWN data=0 size=4[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9] lock=(77|0) plock=0 flags=0000 heap=0 block=\? owner=M:THP$
^ours: UNKNOWN data=0 size=(48|32) lock=77 plock=(0|1) flags=0000 heap=0 block=\? owner=T:self$
# other modules' blocks: w16's KERNEL, USER and GDI are not Microsoft's
# (their segments, sizes; w16's KERNEL's code is not in the global heap)
^GlobalEntryHandle\((BurgerMaster|USER|USER DS|KERNEL)\)
^GlobalEntryModule\((USER|KERNEL|GDI),1\)
# ---- local heaps (section l) ----
# GDI's objects: w16's GDI keeps a region in three blocks of its heap,
# 3.1's in two
^GDI heap type (6|15)
# ---- modules (section m) ----
# the modules Windows loads, their usage counts, KERNEL's file name: 3.1
# loads more fonts; the reference's KRNL286 is run as KRNL386.EXE
^module (KERNEL|SYSTEM|KEYBOARD|MOUSE|DISPLAY|SOUND|COMM|GDI|USER|FONTS|OEMFONTS|FIXFONTS|SSERIFE|COURE|SERIFE|SYMBOLE|ROMAN|SCRIPT|MODERN|SMALLE)
^Module(FindName|FindHandle)\(KERNEL\)
# ---- classes (section c) ----
# USER's classes: w16's USER registers its own set, in its own order, and
# keeps them in its DGROUP (UserSeeUserDo request 4); 3.1's elsewhere
^UserSeeUserDo\(4\)
^class
^classes:
# ---- stack traces (section s) ----
# a waiting task's frames in KERNEL and USER: other code (the child's own
# frames are compared)
^child trace [0-9]+: 1 task=T:child ss=child.dg.s cs=\?\((KRNL386|KRNL286|USER|\?)\)
s/^child trace [0-9]+: (1 task=T:child ss=child.dg.s cs=\?\(THC\))/child trace: \1/
# ---- notifications (sections n, x, k) ----
# NFYLOADSEG.wType: the segment's flags in the module table, whose high
# bits w16's KERNEL keeps otherwise (bit 0400h: UNVERIFIED meaning)
s/ type=[0-9]+ inst=/ type=* inst=/
# the order in which a program's segments are freed when it ends: w16's
# KERNEL frees them in segment order
s/FREESEG (THC\.seg[0-9]|M:THC|child\.dg\.s|\?) task=/FREESEG (THC) task=/
# ---- task control (section k) ----
# the frame the scheduler leaves (TaskGetCSIP's address at 12h, BP at 10h
# are compared through the functions): its other words are KERNEL's own
# registers; 3.1's scheduler has a task's TDB in DS when it switches
^child frame
s/ ds=(T:child|\?)$/ ds=*/
# TaskGetCSIP: the scheduler's code, in KERNEL, which w16 does not keep in
# the global heap (no module name for it)
s/^TaskGetCSIP\(child\): cs=(KRNL386|KRNL286|\?) /TaskGetCSIP(child): cs=KERNEL /
# ---- information (section i) ----
# DPMI's largest free block: DOSX's (3.1 under KRNL286: 0) or w16's
^MemManInfo: largest==
# ---- section d: counts ----
^d (walk|modules|classes|stack frames|tasks)
# ---- KRNL386 only ----
# a code segment without relocations (THP's fourth) gets 20h bytes more
# under 3.1's KRNL386 than w16's gives it (UNVERIFIED why)
^ours: CODE data=4 size=(224|256) 
^GlobalEntryModule\(own,4\): 1 CODE data=4 size=(224|256) 
# a locked RCDATA resource: under w16's KRNL386 the block's handle is not
# the one LoadResource returned (UNVERIFIED: which form w16 gives)
^GlobalEntryHandle\(RCDATA locked\)
